{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '220,240p;2235,2255p;2433,2452p;2660,2690p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '180,198p;380,397p;640,657p;3490,3535p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; rg -n 'exit persists|recovery.*body|both fenced|child.*profile|custody-(epoch|lease|holder)|version-float|version-absent|no-tag|version-2' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc; sed -n '1420,1452p;1705,1728p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\nreaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\nspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\npermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\nsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n\n### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\nVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\ngate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\none. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\nleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\nsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\nThe wire unit is the draft's:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n```\nengine takes.\") with: \"Loading a fetched vector has **one path**: the\ndirect decode of the canonical vector into an image through the one\nvalidator (`yin.vm.semantic-register.code/well-formed?`). The projection\npath (vector → datoms → loader) is retired (owner ruling 2026-09-18;\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §3.4). The conformance obligation is\nthat the two AST projection lanes and the direct vector load yield one\nimage and address (`load_paths_test.cljc`).\"\n\n**U2 — §7.4.1 (ACCEPT).** At its head: \"Superseded for the\nregister-shaped machine by `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §4.1 (two\nstate columns, `act` and `tail`, and the `deliver` record); the table\nbelow describes the stack-shaped machine, which leaves at that design's\nphase 8.\" Replace the `:yin.k/frame` example with §1.2's keyed form.\n\n**U3 — §7.4.2 (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace, in the opening\nparagraph, \"absolute stack depth and environment key sets at a pc depend\non the *activation* — a closure enters with the caller's residual\noperand stack and an environment formed from its captured environment\nplus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack heights\nat joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths\" with\n\"the saved window's values, the environment's bindings and the depth of\nreads: §7.2.1 (versions 0 and 1, `:166-321`), the version-0/1 custody\ngate and fork rules (`:234-290`), §7.4.3 r5's version-0 notes (`:966-970`:\n\"A version-0 entry without a phase reads as `:running`\", \"The landed\nversion-0 reader breaches both\"), §7.7.4's `nil`-header version-0 lift\nand recovery of legacy fork bytes (`:1781`, `:1806-1807`, `:1844`), and\n§7.11.1's version-0/1 interoperation rows (`:3051-3057`, `:3095-3096`).\nInsert at the head of §7.2.1: \"**Scope (2026-10-11).** Body versions 0,\n1 and 2 and every clause of this document that admits, reads, recovers\nor interoperates with them are **historical**: they describe the\nstack-shaped machine's handoff, which leaves at\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §10 phase 8. The row carrier of body\nversion 3 (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`) reads no other version and\ntakes **precedence** over any sentence below that implies otherwise; a\nconflict is resolved in its favour without further amendment. **Not\nhistorical**: the custody, fencing and operation-id semantics (§7.7.8,\n§7.9, §7.4.3's operation ids) travel in version 3 as the root's\n`custody` slot (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5) and stay\noperative; where those sections say 'a version-1 body' read, for version\n3, 'a root whose custody policy is `:yin.k/exclusive`', and where they\n**Revision 5 (after 6c) adds the three questions the rulings left\n(`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`); they are\nnumbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n\n- **6c Q1, the native-lane matrix scope (changes acceptance counts).\n  ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13.** The de Bruijn lanes run the\n  shared fragment: law 4 on 61 of the 89 cases, law 5 on 350 of the 534\n  transfers, tail acceptance on rows 6 and 20 only.\n  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner retains the backends after phase 8 on the same fragment.\n  The canonical lane keeps the full matrix; the retained backends keep\n  this fragment (§4.6).\n  The owner confirmed the matrix of 61 law-4 cases and 350 law-5\n  transfers as normative; §5.3 and §8.3 items 10-11 state it so.\n- **6c Q2, the retained-FFI seam (kernel edit, own slice). Open.** Land\n  6c-post-D2, the four-line response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels\n  (§4.3; half a day), raising law 5 to 366 and 96 and emptying\n  `unlowerable`.\n  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: kernel retention does not widen the fragment.\n  The question remains open; the phase-7 ruling retains the current\n  fragment, so these extra transfers are not acceptance claims.\n- **6c Q3, closures in the free environment (information; a decision\n  only if the owner disagrees). Open.** Closures and continuations in\n  the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (§6.3,\n  R3) and admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de\n  Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3\n  becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n\nunchanged under its own dispatch key `:dao.stream/outcome`: for those\ndirect failures UCF never renames it or nests it under a private key.\nAdmission outcomes are a second, disjoint family with their own dispatch\nkey. The full algebra is in 7.9.\n\n### 7.2.1 The handoff body: the task wire grammar, versions 0 and 1\n\n**Scope (2026-10-11).** Body versions 0, 1 and 2 and every clause of\nthis document that admits, reads, recovers or interoperates with them\nare **historical**: they describe the stack-shaped machine's handoff,\nwhich leaves at `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §10 phase 8. The row\ncarrier of body version 3 (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`) reads no other version and\ntakes **precedence** over any sentence below that implies otherwise; a\nconflict is resolved in its favour without further amendment. **Not\nhistorical**: the custody, fencing and operation-id semantics (§7.7.8,\n§7.9, §7.4.3's operation ids) travel in version 3 as the root's\n`custody` slot (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5) and stay\noperative; where those sections say \"a version-1 body\" read, for version\nits §3.4 (`yin.vm.semantic-register.code/well-formed?`); those sections\ntake precedence over this one wherever the two differ, without further\namendment. What survives of this section for `\"v4\"` is the identity\nlayer: the address is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` computed over the\nvector alone, the header folds away, provenance and the hash itself are\nexcluded, no ordering is imposed, and equal addresses mean equal\nresolved instruction streams and nothing more. The rest of this section\n(the stack-shaped vector, the §2.4 opcode table, the batch-loader\nresolved interpretation, saturation from a batch and the normalization\ngate) describes the `\"v3\"` stack-shaped machine's vector and is\n**historical**: that machine leaves at the frozen design's §10 phase 8,\nand a `\"v4\"` vector is never computed from a datom batch by the\nprocedure below (the two AST projection lanes of §7.3.4 produce it from\nthe tree). The vector→datoms projection is retired (§7.3.4).\n\nThe segment attribute `:yin.code/hash`, reserved in `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n§2.2, becomes required on any segment a continuation may reference. Its\nvalue is the `dao.jing` segment address of the segment's **canonical\nsnapshot variant and every retry of the same park (§7.7.2). A re-emission\nafter a failed publication reuses the occurrence; it never mints a second\nrunnable checkpoint.\n\n## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\n\n### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\n\nSuperseded for the register-shaped machine by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §4.1 (two state columns, `act` and\n`tail`, and the `deliver` record); the table below describes the\nstack-shaped machine, which leaves at that design's phase 8.\n\nThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\nInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\ninside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\nthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\nUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:244:The spellings `:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/response-cell` and `:yin.k/c-<n>` are the body-version 0/1/2 wire spellings, as the version-2 code (`ucf/handoff.cljc`, `ucf/remote.cljc`) uses them; version 3, the row carrier of `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, spells the same cursor cells `:yin.k/cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/cursor-cell`, `:yin.k/response-cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:851:(`:yin.k/cell`, `:yin.k/response-cell` and `:yin.k/c-<n>` are the version-2 wire spellings; version 3 spells them `:yin.k/cursor-cell`, `:yin.k/response-cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, 7.2.1.)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:857:`:yin.k/registers R` of the version-2 amendment is the `act`/`tail` state\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1377:the cursor cell a \"cell\"; the version-2 handoff carrier keeps its shipped\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1398:(Body versions 0/1/2 spell the table `:yin.k/cells`, the reference key `:yin.k/cell` and the ids `:yin.k/c-<n>`, exactly as the version-2 code does; version 3 spells them `:yin.k/cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/cursor-cell` and `:yin.k/cc-<n>`, 7.2.1.)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1984:The driver's exit persists recovery and body, journals the fence, attempts\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:3244:   version or a float `3.0`, or corrupt a child task's profile. Carrier\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:231:  (doseq [n [\"op-id-at-counter\" \"custody-epoch\" \"missing-policy\" \"fork-op-id\" \"child-header\"]]\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:243:   (m \"version-float\" \"successor\" #(assoc % :yin.k/version (cbor/float64 3))\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:245:   (m \"version-2\" \"successor\" #(assoc % :yin.k/version 2)\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:247:   (m \"version-absent\" \"successor\" #(dissoc % :yin.k/version)\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:249:   (m \"no-tag\" \"successor\" #(dissoc % :yin.k/handoff)\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:304:   (m \"custody-epoch\" \"successor\" (custody-set :yin.k/epoch 0)\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:306:   (m \"custody-lease\" \"successor\" (custody-set :dao.lease/lease \"lease-7\")\n      (is (zero? none) \"refused before any attachment\")\n      (is (= [:yin.k/unsatisfied #{'scoped}] [(:yin.k/status r) (get-in r [:yin.k/missing :yin.k/stores])])))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Phase 6c: the cross-engine lanes (§5.3, §5.4 laws 4 and 5, §8.3 10-12)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def link-rows\n  \"Rows the de Bruijn lanes do not carry (§5.3, the shared fragment): a\n   de Bruijn kernel links a native image while module responses carry A,\n   and §4 defines no layout for install children (canonical-task refuses\n   :installs). The reference lane runs the whole matrix; these lanes are\n   removed with their kernels at phase 8.\"\n  #{7 8 9 10 11 12 17})\n\n\n(def lane-rows\n  \"The wrapped rows every lane executes: 15 of §5.2's 22.\"\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) rows))\n\n\n(defn lane-cases\n  \"§5.3's shared fragment: 15 lane rows x 4 segments, plus row 20 once:\n   [row segment expected build].\"\n  []\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) (matrix-cases)))\n\n\n(defn on-lane\n  [lane build]\n  (binding [*lane* lane] (build)))\n\n\n\n(def ordered-pairs\n  \"Law 5's six ordered pairs of distinct lanes.\"\n  (into [] (mapcat (fn [from] (keep (fn [to] (when (not= from to) [from to])) lanes))) lanes))\n\n\n(def unlowerable\n  \"[row receiver] pairs a de Bruijn receiver cannot continue (§5.3): row\n   4 is lifted from a de Bruijn source but not lowered into a de Bruijn\n   receiver, whose re-park names its own call pair\n   (ffi/response-wait-entry), not the carried response cursor cell the\n   semantic-register kernel restores. Emptied when the retained-FFI seam\n   (§4, 6c-post-D2) lands, raising law 5 to 366 and 96.\"\n  #{[4 :stack] [4 :register]})\n\n\n(defn crossed\n  \"Lift on from, lower into a fresh receiver on to, satisfy through the\n   source's streams: the run's observables and attaches.\"\n  [from to build]\n  (scoped\n    (fn []\n      (let [world (on-lane from build)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
